{"qas": [
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2410.04772, why is black-box access considered minimally necessary for AI audits, and what are its limitations compared to stronger access?", "answer": "Black-box access is minimally necessary because it provides concrete evidence about the deployed AI system's behavior, but it does not reveal developers' reasoning or intent, which may require stronger access.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "Of the four options considered in this section, only access to the trained model circumvents the problem of \"underspecification\" or \"model multiplicity\" [17, 40]. This implies that access to the trained model is minimally necessary for meaningful audits because it best indicates how the system behaves when deployed."}, {"page": 15, "quote": "An auditor cannot gain insight into the developers' process and reasoning from the trained model alone, which is not always satisfactory from a broader accountability perspective. Much of US law, for instance, considers intent when determining culpability."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2410.04772, how does the model skeleton differ from the training procedure in terms of what an auditor can learn?", "answer": "The model skeleton reveals system interfaces and component relationships, while the training procedure details optimization and design choices; both allow sanity checks but have different focuses.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "From the model skeleton, an auditor can determine the expected inputs (e.g., types of features) and outputs (e.g., a floating point number between 0 and 1) of the model. The auditor can also ascertain how many components make up the AI system and the relationship between different components of the AI system."}, {"page": 10, "quote": "the training procedure, which includes details such as the general model class (e.g., decision trees, linear models, transformers), objective function used to optimize parameters, hyperparameter tuning methodology, quality checks, or model selection criteria."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2410.04772, what does the null hypothesis represent in the context of auditing under NYC Local Law 144?", "answer": "The null hypothesis represents the presumption that the algorithm is compliant unless evidence is sufficient to reject this presumption.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "u1D43B0 is known as the null hypothesis. In practice, the implication is that the auditor's presumption under (1) is that u1D453 is compliant. The auditor therefore assumes (and reports) that u1D453 is compliant unless the evidence allows them to confidently reject this presumption."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2410.04772, what is the definition of a u1D454-compliant algorithm?", "answer": "An algorithm u1D453 is u1D454-compliant if and only if u1D454(u1D453 ) ≤ 0.", "evidence": [{"page": 13, "quote": "Definition 1. We say that an algorithm u1D453 ∈ F is u1D454-compliant if and only if u1D454(u1D453 ) ≤ 0."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2410.04772, what challenge arises from the use of 'statistical significance' in NYC Local Law 144 audits?", "answer": "Statistical significance is asymmetric and only applies to rejecting the null hypothesis, so requiring it may be problematic if the null is true.", "evidence": [{"page": 15, "quote": "However, \"statistical significance\" generally applies only to the ability to reject the null hypothesis; one cannot obtain a statistically significant confirmation of the null hypothesis. As such, \"statistical significance\" cannot be achieved if one adopts the null hypothesis in (1) and the null hypothesis is indeed true."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2410.04772, what is a benefit of black-box audits being model agnostic?", "answer": "Black-box audits do not require adaptation to the underlying AI system, enabling standardized procedures across different systems.", "evidence": [{"page": 12, "quote": "Perhaps the key benefit is that the audit does not need to be adapted to the underlying AI system, making it possible to develop a standardized audit procedure that works across multiple AI systems."}]}
]}
